      "problem": "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=-5.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+2\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
